\title{Data Types: Categories and Defaults}
\description{
For promotion, every data type belongs to one of three categories, ordered
boolean < integer < float:
\itemize{
\item \strong{boolean} -- \code{bool}
\item \strong{integer} -- \code{i8}, \code{i16}, \code{i32}, \code{i64} and their unsigned counterparts
\code{ui8}, \code{ui16}, \code{ui32}, \code{ui64}
\item \strong{float} -- \code{f32}, \code{f64}
}

Documentation names a group of them with a single word: \emph{any} data type is
all of them, \emph{numeric} is integer and float, \emph{integerish} is boolean and
integer, \emph{signed numeric} is signed integer and float.

These are the categories promotion works in, where signed and unsigned
integers count as one. \code{\link[tengen:dtype_category]{tengen::dtype_category()}} reports a finer split
that names \code{int} and \code{uint} separately.
}
\section{Default Data Types}{

An R value has no data type of its own. Where nothing in the program says
which one it should take, it commits to the default of its R storage type: a
\code{double} becomes \code{f32}, an \code{integer} becomes \code{i32}, a \code{logical} becomes
\code{bool}. \code{\link[=peek_dtype]{peek_dtype()}} reports the default an R value would commit to.

Within its own category an R value assumes the data type it meets instead,
and is built at it directly rather than converted to it, which is what keeps
\code{nv_scalar(1, "f64") / sqrt(2)} exact. The primitives require operands that
have a data type to agree on it; the \verb{nv_*} functions promote them to a
common one.
}

